{"timestamp":"2026-06-12T08:22:54.099+05:30","incident_id":"inc-2580d3ce75","service":"auth-api","status":"COMPLETED","severity":"MEDIUM","root_cause":"Intermittent auth-api latency regression affecting /token/refresh and /session requests, with the strongest evidence on auth-02 /token/refresh. The observed regressions are not explained by CPU, memory, or database connection pool saturation; affected requests returned HTTP 200 and were associated with redis and identity_provider dependencies across deployment versions 2026.06.1 and 2026.06.3.","confidence_score":0.74,"evidence_summary":"Splunk evidence for auth-api between 2026-06-12 08:14:00 and 08:21:00 IST showed 19 auth-api events, 3 latency_regression WARN events, max latency 1534 ms, and average latency 371.05 ms. Regression events occurred at 08:16:01 IST on /session auth-01 with redis latency 1522 ms, 08:17:36 IST on /token/refresh auth-02 with identity_provider latency 938 ms, and 08:19:02 IST on /token/refresh auth-02 with redis latency 1534 ms. Resource metrics during the largest spike were low to moderate: cpu_pct 31.04, memory_pct 41.14, db_connection_pool_pct 27.08. No 5xx errors were observed in the returned incident evidence; status_code was 200 for latency regression events.","ai_summary":"The incident is best characterized as a latency-only degradation in auth-api rather than a hard outage. Evidence points to intermittent dependency or request-path latency on /token/refresh and /session, concentrated around auth-02 and redis or identity_provider calls. Infrastructure saturation is unlikely based on available CPU, memory, and DB pool metrics. Because multiple services also showed latency_regression events in the surrounding window, shared dependency health should be checked before making service-specific changes.","llm_provider":"codex_mcp_agent","mcp_evidence_summary":"MCP client call: MCP evidence for auth-api: 1 events; errors=aiops-incidents; hosts=CENTRAL, unknown-host; endpoints=unknown endpoi